This '''outline of artificial intelligence''' organizes the field into foundations, problem solving, learning, representation, agents and applications. It provides a structured route through AI rather than a list of individual algorithms.

== Foundations and major branches ==
* Foundations: [[Computation]], [[Algorithm]], [[Computational complexity]], [[Probability]], [[Decision theory]], [[Logic]], optimization and statistics.
* Agents: [[Intelligent agent]], [[Autonomous agent (artificial intelligence)]], environment models, perception, action, objectives and feedback.
* Problem solving: state-space search, heuristics, constraint satisfaction, [[Planning (artificial intelligence)]], game-tree search.
* Knowledge and reasoning: [[Knowledge representation]], [[First-order logic]], [[Description logic]], uncertainty, probabilistic inference and causal models.
* Learning: [[Machine learning]], supervised and unsupervised methods, reinforcement learning, representation learning, neural networks and generative models.
* Multi-agent and distributed methods: [[Multi-agent system]], [[Distributed artificial intelligence]], [[Swarm intelligence]], communication and coordination.
* Applications: natural language processing, computer vision, speech, robotics, recommendation and scientific discovery.
* Evaluation and governance: [[Generalization (machine learning)]], [[Data leakage (machine learning)]], robustness, safety, interpretability, privacy and human oversight.

== Conceptual boundaries ==
AI is broader than machine learning; a search or reasoning system need not train a statistical model. A trained model need not be an autonomous agent. An architecture can combine symbolic and statistical methods. The field's organization is historically contingent and branches overlap. For evaluation, specify task distributions, objective functions, data provenance, human roles and operational risks. Performance on a benchmark is not evidence of subjective experience. The [[List of artificial intelligence algorithms]] is a method-level index; this outline instead organizes problems, principles and research areas.

